                "content": "**Notation**\n\n*C1* \u2013 carbon that carries the bromine  \n*C2* \u2013 carbon that carries the tert\u2011butyl group  \n\nThe two possible chair conformations of a cyclohexane ring are called **chair\u202fA** (the \u201clow\u2011energy\u201d chair) and **chair\u202fB** (the ring\u2011flipped, higher\u2011energy chair).  \nFor any substituent X the free\u2011energy cost of placing it **axial** instead of **equatorial** is  \n\n\\[\nA(X)=\\begin{cases}\n2.00\\;\\text{kJ mol}^{-1}&\\text{for Br}\\\\[2pt]\n22.00\\;\\text{kJ mol}^{-1}&\\text{for tert\u2011Bu}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAll conformational free energies are taken as additive and the two chairs interconvert rapidly.\n\nThe thermal energy at \\(T=298.15\\ \\text{K}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nRT = (8.3144626\\ \\text{J mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1})(298.15\\ \\text{K})\n      = 2.4776\\ \\text{kJ mol}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (1)   Chair assignments for the two stereoisomers  \n\n| Stereoisomer | Chair\u202fA (lower\u2011energy) | Chair\u202fB (higher\u2011energy) |\n|--------------|------------------------|--------------------------|\n| **cis\u20111\u2011bromo\u20112\u2011tert\u2011butyl\u2011cyclohexane** | **Br\u202faxial**, **tert\u2011Bu\u202fequatorial** (\u0394G =\u202f+2\u202fkJ\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9) | **Br\u202fequatorial**, **tert\u2011Bu\u202faxial** (\u0394G =\u202f+22\u202fkJ\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9) |\n| **trans\u20111\u2011bromo\u20112\u2011tert\u2011butyl\u2011cyclohexane** | **Br\u202fequatorial**, **tert\u2011Bu\u202fequatorial** (\u0394G =\u202f0) | **Br\u202faxial**, **tert\u2011Bu\u202faxial** (\u0394G =\u202f+24\u202fkJ\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9) |\n\nIn a chair, axial bonds on neighbouring carbons are anti\u2011periplanar; therefore an **axial C\u2013Br** is required for an E2 step, and the \u03b2\u2011hydrogen (or deuterium) must be axial on the adjacent carbon.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (2)   Populations of the E2\u2011reactive chairs  \n\nThe Boltzmann factor for a state of free\u2011energy \\(\\Delta G\\) is  \n\n\\[\nw=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta G}{RT}\\right).\n\\]\n\n#### Cis isomer  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_A &=\\exp\\!\\bigl(-2.00/2.4776\\bigr)=0.447\\\\\nw_B &=\\exp\\!\\bigl(-22.00/2.4776\\bigr)=1.44\\times10^{-4}\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{total }w &=0.447+1.44\\times10^{-4}=0.44714\\\\[2pt]\nf_{\\text{reactive}}&=\n\\frac{w_A}{w_A+w_B}=0.9997\\;(99.97\\%)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The reactive chair is the one with Br\u202faxial.)\n\n#### Trans isomer  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_A &=\\exp(0)=1.000\\\\\nw_B &=\\exp\\!\\bigl(-24.00/2.4776\\bigr)=6.66\\times10^{-5}\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{total }w &=1.00007\\\\[2pt]\nf_{\\text{reactive}}&=\n\\frac{w_B}{w_A+w_B}=6.66\\times10^{-5}\\;(0.0067\\%)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The reactive chair is the high\u2011energy one with Br\u202faxial.)\n\nBecause the intrinsic first\u2011order E2 rate constant once a molecule is in a reactive chair (\\(k_{\\text{int}}\\)) is assumed identical for the two stereoisomers,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\frac{k_{\\text{obs,cis}}}{k_{\\text{obs,trans}}}\n= \\frac{f_{\\text{reactive,cis}}}{f_{\\text{reactive,trans}}}\n\\approx \\frac{0.9997}{6.66\\times10^{-5}}\n\\simeq 1.5\\times10^{4}}\n\\]\n\nThus the cis compound reacts roughly fifteen thousand times faster than the trans compound under the given conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (3)   Product distribution from the cis reactive chair (no isotope)\n\nFrom the axial\u2011Br chair the two possible \u03b2\u2011hydrogens are at C2 and C6.  \nIntrinsic rates (given)  \n\n\\[\nk_{1,2}:k_{1,6}=4.00:1.00 .\n\\]\n\nSince both pathways are available from the same highly populated chair, the product ratio follows directly from the intrinsic rates:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\frac{[\\text{C1=C2}]}{[\\text{C1=C6}]} = 4:1 }\n\\]\n\nThe more substituted alkene (C1=C2) is the major product.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (4)   Effect of deuterating the axial \u03b2\u2011hydrogen at C2  \n\nOnly the axial hydrogen on C2 (the one that would lead to C1=C2) is replaced by deuterium.  \nPrimary kinetic isotope effect  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k_H}{k_D}=6.0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk_{1,2,D}= \\frac{k_{1,2,H}}{6}= \\frac{4}{6}=0.667 .\n\\]\n\nThe rate for the C1=C6 pathway is unchanged (\\(k_{1,6}=1\\)).  \nHence the new relative rates are  \n\n\\[\nk_{1,2,D}:k_{1,6}=0.667:1.00 = 2:3 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the product distribution reverses:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\frac{[\\text{C1=C2}]}{[\\text{C1=C6}]}\\approx 0.67:1\\;(2:3)}\n\\]\n\nThe less\u2011substituted alkene (C1=C6) now dominates, showing that the \u201cmore\u2011substituted\u2011alkene\u2011is\u2011favoured\u2019\u2019 rule can be overridden by a strong primary isotope effect.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (5)   Why elimination to C2 is impossible in the trans reactive chair  \n\nIn the trans isomer the only E2\u2011reactive chair is the high\u2011energy one in which **both** Br and tert\u2011butyl are axial.  \nOn C2 the tert\u2011butyl occupies the axial position; consequently the hydrogen attached to C2 resides in the **equatorial** orientation.  \n\nFor an E2 process the \u03b2\u2011hydrogen must be **axial** and anti\u2011periplanar to the axial C\u2013Br bond. Because the C2 hydrogen is equatorial, the necessary anti\u2011periplanar geometry is not achieved, so removal of Br together with that hydrogen cannot occur.  \n\nThe only axial \u03b2\u2011hydrogen available in this chair is the one on C6. Therefore elimination can proceed only to give the **C1=C6** double bond.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (6)   Why \u201cthe more\u2011substituted alkene is favoured in E2\u2019\u2019 is insufficient  \n\n1. **Conformational population effects** \u2013  \n   The two stereoisomers do not sample the same set of chairs equally.  \n   *Cis* spends >\u202f99\u202f% of the time in a chair that places Br axial, whereas *trans* spends <\u202f0.01\u202f% of the time in such a chair. Consequently the overall observable rate (and therefore the amount of product formed) is dictated first by how many molecules are in a reactive conformation.\n\n2. **Stereoelectronic (anti\u2011periplanar) requirement** \u2013  \n   An E2 transition state demands an axial C\u2013Br and an axial \u03b2\u2011hydrogen.  \n   Even if a \u03b2\u2011hydrogen is present on a neighbouring carbon, it must be axial and correctly oriented. In the trans reactive chair the \u03b2\u2011hydrogen at C2 is equatorial, so that pathway is geometrically forbidden despite the fact that the resulting alkene would be more substituted.\n\n3. **Intrinsic alkene\u2011forming preferences** \u2013  \n   When the correct geometry is available, the intrinsic electronic stabilization of the transition state (and of the product) makes the more\u2011substituted alkene intrinsically faster (here 4\u202f:\u202f1). However this intrinsic bias can be outweighed by kinetic isotope effects (as shown in part\u202f4) or by the scarcity of the appropriate conformer (part\u202f5).\n\nTherefore a complete prediction must combine **(i)** the Boltzmann\u2011weighted population of E2\u2011reactive chairs, **(ii)** the stereoelectronic necessity of an axial\u2011axial anti\u2011periplanar arrangement, and **(iii)** the intrinsic relative rates of forming the different alkenes. Ignoring any of these aspects leads to an incorrect conclusion, as illustrated by the dramatic difference between the cis and trans substrates and by the reversal of product distribution upon deuterium substitution.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multiple subquestions about E2 elimination from cis and trans 1-bromo-2-tert-butylcyclohexane.\n\nGiven A(Br)=2 kJ/mol penalty when axial vs eq. A(tBu)=22 kJ/mol penalty axial vs eq.\n\nAssume additive conformational free energies and rapid chair-chair interconversion.\n\nGoal:\n\n(1) Identify lower-energy chair conformation and ring-flipped conformation for each stereoisomer, specifying axial/equatorial positions.\n\nWe have cis-1-bromo-2-tert-butylcyclohexane: meaning substituents on same face relative to ring. In cis configuration, they are either both axial in one chair and both equatorial in other? Let's think: In cyclohexane chairs, axial positions alternate up/down each carbon. If we label C1 with Br, C2 with tBu. For cis arrangement, if Br is axial at C1, then the axial direction at C2 will be opposite (since axial alternates). So axial at C1 points up, axial at C2 points down. Therefore they cannot both be axial simultaneously because they'd be on opposite sides. To be cis, they must be both axial in same orientation? Actually cis means they are on same side of the ring (both pseudo-equatorial?). In chair, you can have both axial but pointing opposite directions (one up, one down) - that's trans. So cis would be one axial, one equatorial. Indeed typical textbook: cis-1-substituted-2-substituted cyclohexane has one axial, one equatorial in each chair. So for cis, there are two chair conformations: (i) Br axial, tBu equatorial; (ii) Br equatorial, tBu axial. Which is lower energy depends on penalties: axial Br penalty 2 kJ, axial tBu penalty 22 kJ. So compare energies:\n\nConformation A: Br axial (+2), tBu eq (0) => \u0394G = +2 kJ.\nConformation B: Br eq (0), tBu axial (+22) => \u0394G = +22 kJ.\n\nThus lower-energy chair is with Br axial, tBu equatorial (less penalty). The higher-energy chair is the flipped version where they swap.\n\nSimilarly for trans-1-bromo-2-tert-butylcyclohexane: trans means substituents on opposite faces. That corresponds to both axial (pointing opposite directions) or both equatorial (both same orientation). So two chairs: (i) Both axial (Br axial up, tBu axial down) \u2013 both incur axial penalties: 2+22=24 kJ. (ii) Both equatorial (Br eq, tBu eq) \u2013 zero penalty. So lower-energy chair is both equatorial (\u0394G=0). Ring-flipped conformation would be both axial (higher energy).\n\nThus answer (1):\n\nCis: lower-energy chair: Br axial, tBu equatorial. Flipped chair: Br equatorial, tBu axial.\n\nTrans: lower-energy chair: both equatorial. Flipped chair: both axial.\n\nNow (2): Need fraction of molecules in E2-reactive conformation for each stereoisomer.\n\nReactive conformation defined as having axial C-Br (necessary) and axial \u03b2-H/D anti-periplanar to it. For E2, need anti-periplanar relationship between leaving group (axial) and \u03b2 hydrogen (axial on adjacent carbon). In cyclohexane chair, axial bonds are parallel; the \u03b2 carbon adjacent to C1 is C2 (beta) and also C6 (other direction). Axial H at C2 or C6 anti-periplanar to axial Br at C1? Yes, if Br axial at C1, then axial H at C2 (or C6) are anti-periplanar (they point opposite directions). However need to check orientation: At C1 axial up, at C2 axial down (alternating). Are those anti? In chair, axial at C1 and axial at C2 are anti (180\u00b0 dihedral). So yes.\n\nThus for cis isomer, the lower-energy chair already has Br axial, tBu equatorial, and axial \u03b2 H's at C2 and C6 are present (both axial). So that chair is reactive. The flipped chair (Br eq, tBu axial) does not have axial Br, so not reactive.\n\nThus fraction of molecules in reactive conformation = population of lower-energy chair / total population of both chairs.\n\nCompute Boltzmann distribution: weight proportional to exp(-\u0394G/RT). Let G_lower = 2 kJ (for cis lower-energy). G_higher = 22 kJ (cis flipped). Use R=8.314 J/mol K, T=298.15 K.\n\nCalculate RT = 8.314*298.15 \u2248 2478 J = 2.478 kJ/mol.\n\nPopulation fractions:\n\ncis lower: exp(-2/2.478) = exp(-0.807) = 0.447.\ncis higher: exp(-22/2.478) = exp(-8.842) = 0.000145.\n\nTotal = 0.447 + 0.000145 = 0.447145.\n\nFraction reactive = lower/(total) = 0.447/0.447145 \u2248 0.9997 ~ 99.97%.\n\nEssentially essentially all molecules are in reactive conformation.\n\nFor trans isomer: lower-energy chair both equatorial (\u0394G=0). This chair has Br equatorial, not axial \u2192 not reactive. Higher-energy chair both axial (\u0394G=24 kJ). This chair has axial Br and axial \u03b2 H at C2 and C6 (both axial). So reactive conformation is the higher-energy chair.\n\nCompute populations:\n\ntrans low (0 kJ): weight = exp(0)=1.\ntrans high (24 kJ): weight = exp(-24/2.478)=exp(-9.648)=6.68e-5.\n\nTotal = 1.0000668.\n\nFraction reactive = high/(total) = 6.68e-5 / 1.0000668 \u2248 6.68e-5 (~0.0067%). Very small.\n\nThus fraction reactive cis \u2248 0.9997, trans \u2248 6.7\u00d710\u207b\u2075.\n\nNow define k_int as total first-order E2 rate constant once in reactive chair. Since assumed identical for both stereoisomers, observed rate constants:\n\nk_obs = f_reactive * k_int.\n\nThus ratio k_obs,cis/k_obs,trans = f_cis / f_trans \u2248 0.9997 / 6.68e-5 \u2248 1.496e4 \u2248 1.5 \u00d710^4.\n\nSo cis reacts about fifteen thousand times faster.\n\nNow (3): For cis reactive chair, two eliminations possible: C1=C2 (forming double bond between C1 and C2) and C1=C6 (double bond between C1 and C6). Intrinsic rates ratio k1,2 : k1,6 = 4:1. Assuming conformational interconversion fast, each molecule spends most time in reactive chair (as above). Reaction proceeds via either pathway with probabilities proportional to intrinsic rates. Since both pathways occur from same chair, overall product ratio = 4:1 (more substituted alkene major). So product ratio C1=C2 : C1=C6 = 4:1.\n\nNow (4): Replace only the axial hydrogen at C2 (which is anti-periplanar to axial Br) by deuterium. So now the \u03b2 hydrogen at C2 becomes D. Primary KIE: k_H/k_D = 6.0. Means that elimination using that \u03b2-H (C1-C2) is slowed by factor 6 relative to using H. The other pathway (C1-C6) uses axial H at C6 (unchanged). So intrinsic rates become:\n\nOriginal intrinsic rates: k1,2_H = 4 units, k1,6_H = 1 unit.\n\nAfter D substitution at C2, the rate for C1-C2 pathway reduces by factor 6 (assuming same pre-exponential etc.). So new k1,2_D = (4/6) = 0.667 units. k1,6 remains 1 unit.\n\nThus product ratio = k1,2_D : k1,6 = 0.667 : 1 = 0.667:1 i.e., approx 2:3 (or 1:1.5). Equivalent to 2:3 after scaling multiply by 3 gives 2:3. So C1=C2 product decreased relative to C1=C6. Now C1=C6 becomes major product (ratio 1.5:1). So the more substituted alkene is no longer major; the less substituted (C1=C6) becomes major due to KIE.\n\nThus answer: new ratio ~ 0.67:1 (or 2:3) favoring C1=C6.\n\nNow (5): In trans isomer, the reactive chair is the ring-flipped conformation where both Br and tBu are axial. In that conformation, Br axial at C1, tBu axial at C2. The \u03b2 carbons adjacent to C1 are C2 and C6. At C2, the substituent is tBu (axial). The \u03b2 hydrogen at C2 is actually eclipsed? Wait C2 has tBu axial, but there is also a hydrogen at C2 (since carbon has four substituents: Br at C1, tBu at C2, plus two hydrogens). In the axial position, the tBu occupies axial site, leaving the hydrogen in equatorial position. However for E2 we need axial \u03b2-H anti-periplanar to axial Br. Since the hydrogen at C2 is equatorial, it's not anti-periplanar to axial Br. Therefore elimination toward C2 cannot occur because the required geometry (axial H) is absent. The only axial \u03b2-H available is at C6 (the hydrogen at C6 is axial). So elimination can proceed only to form C1=C6 double bond. So answer accordingly.\n\nExplain: In the axial-axial conformation, the \u03b2 carbon C2 has its hydrogen occupying the equatorial position; thus the anti-periplanar alignment needed for concerted removal of Br and H is not satisfied. Only the axial hydrogen at C6 meets the anti-periplanar requirement, allowing elimination to give the less-substituted alkene (C1=C6).\n\nNow (6): Provide conceptual explanation: The simple rule \"more substituted alkene is favored\" ignores differences in conformational accessibility and stereoelectronic constraints. In our case, cis isomer predominantly exists in a chair where Br is axial and both \u03b2 hydrogens are axial, giving two possible eliminations. Even though the more substituted alkene (C1=C2) is intrinsically preferred (intrinsic rate 4x), its actual yield is modulated by conformational factors. For trans isomer, the reactive conformation is high-energy (both axial), making overall reaction very slow; moreover, only one \u03b2-hydrogen (at C6) is axial, so only the less-substituted alkene can form regardless of substitution preference. Additionally, isotopic substitution changes the effective intrinsic rate, overriding substitution preference. Hence need to consider (a) population of reactive conformers (Boltzmann weighting), (b) stereoelectronic requirement of anti-periplanar arrangement (axial-axial), and (c) intrinsic alkene-forming preference (substituent stabilization). Without accounting for these, the rule fails.\n\nWill craft final answer with clear sections.\n\nLet's produce numeric values precisely maybe to three significant figures.\n\nCompute cis fraction more accurately:\n\nexp(-2/2.477572) = exp(-0.807) = 0.447. Good.\n\nexp(-22/2.477572) = exp(-8.851) = 0.000144.\n\nTotal = 0.447144.\n\nFraction reactive = 0.447 / 0.447144 = 0.999704.\n\nTrans fraction:\n\nexp(-24/2.477572) = exp(-9.647) = 6.66e-5.\n\nTotal = 1.0000666.\n\nFraction reactive = 6.66e-5.\n\nRatio = 0.999704 / 6.66e-5 = 14960 \u2248 1.5\u00d710^4.\n\nProvide numbers.\n\nNow answer (3) product ratio 4:1 (C1=C2 major). (4) after D substitution ratio ~0.67:1 (C1=C6 major). Could express as 2:3.\n\nNow compile final answer."
